1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,079
An old tree trunk gets thicker every year.

2
00:00:03,079 --> 00:00:07,715
A young larkspur has leaves of one
shape, and later grows leaves of another.

3
00:00:08,115 --> 00:00:11,533
And a buttercup grows different
leaves in water and in air.

4
00:00:11,533 --> 00:00:14,535
In each case, plant cells
change what they become.

5
00:00:14,985 --> 00:00:17,426
Diya saw an old tree trunk near college.

6
00:00:17,426 --> 00:00:21,753
She asked: its cells stopped dividing
long ago, so how does it keep getting

7
00:00:21,753 --> 00:00:22,653
thicker?

8
00:00:22,388 --> 00:00:24,972
And how does a cell decide what to become?

9
00:00:25,322 --> 00:00:26,888
Here is the short answer.

10
00:00:26,888 --> 00:00:29,342
Some mature cells can
start dividing again.

11
00:00:29,342 --> 00:00:31,813
And a cell's job depends on where it sits.

12
00:00:32,313 --> 00:00:35,260
By the end, you will know differentiation,
dedifferentiation, redifferentiation,

13
00:00:35,260 --> 00:00:36,324
and how a plant develops.

14
00:00:36,974 --> 00:00:38,620
First, differentiation.

15
00:00:38,970 --> 00:00:42,336
Cells made by meristems grow
up and take on a special job.

16
00:00:42,336 --> 00:00:44,110
This is called differentiation.

17
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:48,458
While differentiating, a cell
changes its walls and its insides.

18
00:00:48,458 --> 00:00:52,779
A water-carrying cell, called a
tracheary element, loses its protoplasm.

19
00:00:52,779 --> 00:00:56,639
It builds a very strong, elastic
wall of cellulose and lignin.

20
00:00:56,639 --> 00:01:00,462
Now it can carry water a long
way, even under great tension.

21
00:01:01,112 --> 00:01:03,360
Let's zoom into a stem to see this.

22
00:01:03,710 --> 00:01:07,449
A young dicot stem has its vascular
bundles arranged in a ring.

23
00:01:07,949 --> 00:01:09,445
Zoom into one bundle.

24
00:01:09,445 --> 00:01:10,833
Xylem carries water.

25
00:01:10,833 --> 00:01:14,961
Between the bundles lies parenchyma,
living cells that store food.

26
00:01:15,461 --> 00:01:16,992
Zoom to one xylem cell.

27
00:01:16,992 --> 00:01:19,827
It is now dead and
empty, with a thick wall.

28
00:01:19,827 --> 00:01:23,383
It has fully differentiated,
and it will never divide again.

29
00:01:24,033 --> 00:01:26,099
So how does a trunk get thicker?

30
00:01:26,099 --> 00:01:27,646
Follow it step by step.

31
00:01:27,996 --> 00:01:28,896
Step one.

32
00:01:28,757 --> 00:01:33,225
Between the bundles, parenchyma cells
are living, mature and not dividing.

33
00:01:33,975 --> 00:01:34,875
Step two.

34
00:01:34,525 --> 00:01:36,463
Some of them get back the power to divide.

35
00:01:36,463 --> 00:01:37,623
This is dedifferentiation.

36
00:01:37,623 --> 00:01:39,265
They form the interfascicular cambium.

37
00:01:40,015 --> 00:01:40,915
Step three.

38
00:01:40,894 --> 00:01:45,011
This joins the cambium inside the
bundles, making a complete ring.

39
00:01:45,761 --> 00:01:46,661
Step four.

40
00:01:46,468 --> 00:01:47,931
The ring makes new cells.

41
00:01:47,931 --> 00:01:51,914
These lose the power to divide again,
and mature to do a job, like carrying

42
00:01:51,914 --> 00:01:52,814
water.

43
00:01:52,410 --> 00:01:53,816
This is redifferentiation.

44
00:01:54,566 --> 00:01:55,466
Step five.

45
00:01:55,339 --> 00:01:59,745
Near the surface, cork cambium also forms
from parenchyma by dedifferentiation.

46
00:01:59,745 --> 00:02:02,205
Year after year, the stem grows thicker.

47
00:02:02,955 --> 00:02:05,144
How does a new cell know what to become?

48
00:02:05,494 --> 00:02:07,841
In plants, differentiation is open.

49
00:02:07,841 --> 00:02:11,155
The same meristem can make
different kinds of cells.

50
00:02:11,605 --> 00:02:15,901
Cells pushed out ahead of the root
apical meristem become root cap cells.

51
00:02:15,901 --> 00:02:18,444
Cells pushed to the side become epidermis.

52
00:02:18,444 --> 00:02:21,509
The final structure depends
on where the cell sits.

53
00:02:22,159 --> 00:02:23,867
Now put it all together.

54
00:02:23,867 --> 00:02:25,262
This is development.

55
00:02:25,612 --> 00:02:29,896
Development means every change in a
plant's life, from a germinating seed

56
00:02:29,896 --> 00:02:31,956
to old age, called senescence.

57
00:02:31,956 --> 00:02:34,112
It is growth plus differentiation.

58
00:02:34,562 --> 00:02:36,974
A cell divides, grows,
differentiates and matures.

59
00:02:36,974 --> 00:02:38,155
Then it ages and dies.

60
00:02:38,155 --> 00:02:41,276
A mature cell can loop back by
dedifferentiation, and divide again.

61
00:02:41,726 --> 00:02:46,233
Development is controlled from inside,
by genes and plant growth regulators.

62
00:02:46,233 --> 00:02:50,636
And from outside, by light, temperature,
water, oxygen and nutrition.

63
00:02:51,286 --> 00:02:55,102
Plants can also take different
paths to make different structures.

64
00:02:55,102 --> 00:02:56,747
This is called plasticity.

65
00:02:57,097 --> 00:03:01,116
In cotton, coriander and larkspur, a
young plant's leaves differ in shape

66
00:03:01,116 --> 00:03:02,831
from a mature plant's leaves.

67
00:03:02,831 --> 00:03:04,731
Here the change comes with age.

68
00:03:05,181 --> 00:03:08,891
In buttercup, leaves made in water
differ from leaves made in air.

69
00:03:08,891 --> 00:03:11,327
Here the change comes
from the surroundings.

70
00:03:11,327 --> 00:03:14,766
Leaves of different shapes on one
plant are called heterophylly.

71
00:03:15,416 --> 00:03:16,316
Your turn.

72
00:03:16,159 --> 00:03:18,475
Cork cambium forms from mature parenchyma.

73
00:03:18,475 --> 00:03:20,995
Is that differentiation,
or dedifferentiation?

74
00:03:20,995 --> 00:03:22,089
Pause and decide.

75
00:03:25,289 --> 00:03:26,189
Dedifferentiation!

76
00:03:26,166 --> 00:03:29,302
A mature cell that was not dividing
gets back the power to divide.

77
00:03:29,952 --> 00:03:30,852
Remember this.

78
00:03:30,791 --> 00:03:33,120
Mature living cells can
become dividers again.

79
00:03:33,720 --> 00:03:35,277
Now, a worked example.

80
00:03:35,277 --> 00:03:37,221
Name the process in each case.

81
00:03:37,571 --> 00:03:38,471
Case one.

82
00:03:38,324 --> 00:03:42,726
A cell from the root tip meristem loses
its protoplasm and becomes a tracheary

83
00:03:42,726 --> 00:03:43,626
element.

84
00:03:43,378 --> 00:03:44,876
That is differentiation.

85
00:03:45,326 --> 00:03:46,226
Case two.

86
00:03:45,978 --> 00:03:48,619
Parenchyma between the
bundles starts dividing again.

87
00:03:48,619 --> 00:03:49,995
That is dedifferentiation.

88
00:03:50,445 --> 00:03:51,345
Case three.

89
00:03:51,209 --> 00:03:54,983
Cells cut off by this new cambium stop
dividing and mature into wood that

90
00:03:54,983 --> 00:03:55,883
carries water.

91
00:03:55,872 --> 00:03:57,310
That is redifferentiation.

92
00:03:57,960 --> 00:03:59,421
Now, the exam trap.

93
00:03:59,771 --> 00:04:03,433
Many students think a mature
plant cell can never divide again.

94
00:04:03,833 --> 00:04:08,117
But living mature cells, like parenchyma,
can regain the power to divide.

95
00:04:08,117 --> 00:04:09,654
That is dedifferentiation.

96
00:04:10,304 --> 00:04:11,379
Where is this asked?

97
00:04:11,379 --> 00:04:14,473
As example questions: which process
forms the interfascicular cambium,

98
00:04:14,473 --> 00:04:16,132
and which plants show heterophylly.

99
00:04:16,732 --> 00:04:17,695
Exam level now.

100
00:04:17,695 --> 00:04:20,078
Which one is an example
of dedifferentiation?

101
00:04:20,078 --> 00:04:21,082
Pause and solve.

102
00:04:26,482 --> 00:04:27,561
The answer is A.

103
00:04:27,561 --> 00:04:30,289
Interfascicular cambium
forms from mature parenchyma.

104
00:04:30,789 --> 00:04:33,672
Mature parenchyma cells
regain the power to divide.

105
00:04:33,672 --> 00:04:35,193
That is dedifferentiation.

106
00:04:35,693 --> 00:04:38,571
Tracheary elements and root cap
cells come from differentiation.

107
00:04:38,571 --> 00:04:40,150
Larkspur leaves show heterophylly.

108
00:04:40,750 --> 00:04:42,291
Here is your recap.

109
00:04:42,291 --> 00:04:43,875
Screenshot this card.

110
00:04:47,075 --> 00:04:49,261
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:04:49,661 --> 00:04:50,561
One.

112
00:04:49,920 --> 00:04:50,820
What is dedifferentiation?

113
00:04:51,701 --> 00:04:52,601
Two.

114
00:04:52,021 --> 00:04:53,403
Give one example of heterophylly.

115
00:04:54,303 --> 00:04:55,203
Three.

116
00:04:54,839 --> 00:04:57,175
Why is plant differentiation called open?

117
00:04:58,275 --> 00:05:01,965
Next time: what tells a plant on a
window sill to bend toward the light?

118
00:05:01,965 --> 00:05:05,123
We find out in plant growth
regulators: auxins and gibberellins.
